response:
Consider the monic polynomial $P(x)=x^3-3x^2+3x-1$. Let $r=\sqrt[3]2$ for simplicity. Then, \begin{align*}(1-r+r^2)^3 &= 1^3-r^3+3r^2(r^2-1)+3(1-r)(r^2-1)^2\\
&=(r-r^3)+3r^2(r-1)+3(r^2-1)[r^2(1-r)-1]\\
&=r-r^3+3r^3-3r^2r+3(r^4-2r^2+1)(-1)\\
&=r-r^3+3r^3-3r^4-3r^4+6r^2-3\\
&=-4r^4+2r^3+6r^2-r-3\\
&=4(2)+2(-3)+6(2)-r-3\\
&=8-6+12-r-3\\
&=11-r.\\
\end{align*} Substituting $x=1$ into $x^3-3x^2+3x-1=0$, we get $1-3+3-1=0$. Therefore, $18P(1-r+r^2)=18P(r^2-r+1)=11-r$. Dividing both sides by $18$, we get $P(1-r+r^2)=\frac{11-r}{18}$. Plugging $r=\sqrt[3]2$ gives $\frac{11-\sqrt[3]2}{18} = \boxed{0}$. From https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2003_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20 Let $r = \sqrt[3]{2}$. We want, as $r \not \in \mathbb{Z}$. We have the polynomial $x^3-3x^2+3x-1$. So we want to quotize $\frac{11-r}{(1+r-r^2)(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}$ to make it integer-valued and minimal degree.

Perform long polynomial division,
$$
\frac{11-r}{(1+r-r^2)(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
=\frac{1-r^4}{(1+r-r^2)(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{1-2r^2}{(1+r-r^2)(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{1-2\sqrt[3]{4}}{(1+\sqrt[3]{2}-\sqrt[3]{4})(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{(1+\sqrt[3]{4})-(3)\sqrt[3]{4}}{(1+\sqrt[3]{2})(1+\sqrt[3]{4}+\sqrt[3]{16})(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{1+\sqrt[3]{4}+3\sqrt[3]{8}}{(1+\sqrt[3]{2})(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{(1+2\sqrt[3]{2})+3\sqrt[3]{4}}{(1+\sqrt[3]{2})(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
\\=\frac{x^3-3x^2+3x-1}{(1+\sqrt[3]{2})(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)}
=1
$$

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{1}$.

